## Feature Design Guidance

### Design around the event lifecycle

Momice covers before, during, and after the event.

Good designs should support:

- Event setup
- Event website
- Registration
- Ticketing
- Communications
- Check-in
- Attendance insight
- Follow-up

Avoid:

- Designing features as isolated forms, emails, or attendee lists
- Ignoring how status changes affect communication and attendance

---

### Make registration status central

Registration status drives planning, communications, access, and reporting.

Good designs should show:

- Registered
- Confirmed
- Waiting list
- Cancelled
- Paid or unpaid where relevant
- Checked in
- No-show
- Online or physical attendee type

Avoid using generic “submitted” states when event-specific status matters.

---

### Connect event emails to attendee status

Event communication should be lifecycle-aware.

Good designs should support:

- Invitations
- Confirmation emails
- Reminder emails
- Access emails
- Updates
- Follow-up emails
- Status-based segments

Avoid treating event emails as unrelated marketing campaigns.

---

### Design check-in for speed and accuracy

Check-in happens under time pressure.

Good designs should support:

- Fast scanning
- Search fallback
- Clear attendee status
- Plus-one visibility
- Badge or access support
- Real-time attendance updates

Avoid complex workflows that slow down on-site staff.

---

### Support multiple event formats

Momice supports physical, online, and hybrid events.

Good designs should show:

- Event format
- Attendee type
- Online access
- On-site check-in
- Format-specific communication
- Attendance tracking

Avoid assuming every event is physical.

---

### Treat event data as follow-up data

Event data should be useful after the event.

Good designs should support:

- Attended vs no-show segmentation
- CRM sync
- Mail+ follow-up
- Event performance insight
- Complete event record

Avoid ending the product workflow at check-in.

---

## Important Assumptions to Preserve

Preserve these assumptions when generating product concepts, UX flows, screens, or feature ideas:

- Momice is event registration software by Spotler.
- It focuses on registration, communication, ticketing, and check-in.
- Event websites, registration forms, emails, attendee data, and check-in are connected.
- Registration status is central.
- Attendance insight is a key product outcome.
- Momice supports physical, online, and hybrid events.
- Paid events and ticketing are supported.
- Plus-ones and waiting lists are supported.
- The check-in app is important for physical event execution.
- Event communications can be triggered or segmented by registration status.
- Online access can be managed for online and hybrid attendees.
- Event data can support CRM and marketing follow-up.
- Spotler CRM and Spotler Mail+ integrations matter.
- External CRM, payment provider, and online event tool integrations may matter.
- The product should reduce manual administration and last-minute stress.
- The product should support repeatable event processes.

---

## Things Figma Make Should Avoid

Avoid these mistakes when designing Momice features:

- Do not treat Momice as only a form builder.
- Do not treat Momice as only an event website builder.
- Do not treat Momice as only a ticketing tool.
- Do not treat event emails as generic newsletter campaigns.
- Do not ignore registration status.
- Do not ignore attendance status.
- Do not ignore check-in workflows for physical events.
- Do not assume every event is physical.
- Do not assume every event is free.
- Do not ignore online and hybrid event access logic.
- Do not treat payment as disconnected from registration.
- Do not ignore plus-ones or waiting lists where relevant.
- Do not end the workflow before post-event follow-up.
- Do not replace Momice terminology with generic SaaS terms like “items”, “records”, “objects”, or “submissions”.
- Do not overfocus on visual styling; that belongs in the Design System Skill.

---

## Edge Cases and Product Logic to Consider

### Attendee registers but does not attend

No-show status matters for reporting and follow-up.

### Attendee attends without prior registration

On-site teams may need to handle walk-ins or manual additions where supported by the event setup.

### Event reaches capacity

Waiting list logic may apply. Designs should support transitions from waiting list to registered.

### Plus-one changes

A main attendee may add, remove, or change a plus-one. Designs should keep related registrations clear.

### Payment incomplete

A paid registration may be started but not completed. Payment status may affect confirmation and access.

### Hybrid attendee changes format

An attendee may switch from physical to online or vice versa. Communications and access may need to update.

### Online access must only go to eligible attendees

Online links should depend on registration status or attendee type.

### Check-in app offline or under pressure

Event-day workflows should be fast and resilient. Search fallback and clear status are important.

### Duplicate registration

An attendee may register more than once. Designs should account for duplicate detection or management where relevant.

### Post-event follow-up differs by status

Attendees, no-shows, online attendees, physical attendees, and waiting-list contacts may require different follow-up.

### CRM sync issue

Registration or attendance data may fail to sync to CRM or Mail+. Designs should show sync status where relevant.

